## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. Your code uses it to kill a thread that exceeds a deadline — this must become cooperative cancellation.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Blocks until task completes or timeout fires
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### For work that truly cooperates (preferred):

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        try
        {
            return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                       .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
                       .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the work keeps running until it checks the token. If `work` is a tight CPU loop that never checks, it won't stop. |
| **Uncooperative work can't be killed** | If you can't modify `work` to accept a `CancellationToken`, no in-process mechanism can force-stop it. Move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill`. |
| **`Task.WaitAsync` ≠ stopping the task** | `WaitAsync` unblocks the caller on timeout, but the underlying task continues running. You must check `token.IsCancellationRequested` inside `work`. |
| **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using` it — leaking causes timer/callback leaks. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (as shown above). |

---

### Removed APIs Summary

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) |
|---------------------|--------------|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(..., token)` |

---

### Bottom Line

The fundamental shift: **preemptive** (runtime injects exception) → **cooperative** (code voluntarily checks). If you control the `work` delegate, pass `CancellationToken` through the entire call chain and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at regular checkpoints. If you don't control it, the only true kill mechanism is `Process.Kill` on a child process.
